# Text adventure assistance: blog working record

Private material for a future post about helping players remember clues and
express commands without replacing the game's interpreter. Collection and review
are ongoing. No model has been trained or evaluated and nothing is published.

- [Experiment journal](experiment-journal.md): decisions, setbacks and receipts.
- [Collection and testing](methodology.md): acquisition, capture, review and export.
- [Model plan](model-plan.md): proposed training and evaluation, not results.
- [Continuous worker pool](worker-pool.md): keeping ten collection games active.
- [Latest evidence pointer](evidence/latest.json): dated counts and archived
  prompt, harness, source-manifest and review-document versions.

At 2026-09-25 02:18 UTC, the first documentation snapshot captured 12,475 events
across ten games. Its separately refreshed CSV held 709 candidate cases: 433
accepted, 165 revise, 15 quarantine and 96 unreviewed. These are inventory counts,
not model performance. Events are not all turns; accepted cases are not all
positive wording repairs. Four endings had been independently verified:
Hitchhiker's Guide, Adventureland, Pirate Adventure and Lost Pig.

## Maintaining the record

Append UTC-dated entries after meaningful changes, citing paths or event IDs.
Retain failed approaches and corrections to our own assumptions. Run from the
experiment root:

```sh
python3 scripts/snapshot_experiment.py --note 'Describe the milestone or correction'
```

The snapshot archives small artifacts as content-addressed objects. Large
append-only journals and raw transcripts remain under `play/`; their exact
prefix lengths and SHA-256 hashes are recorded. Preserve both directories.
This is local evidence preservation, not an off-host backup.

Before publishing, freeze the data/review snapshot, check source permissions,
remove private host/account details and select only necessary game excerpts.
No walkthrough or full transcript is automatically cleared for redistribution.

## Proposed post sequence

1. Why remembering clues and finding parser wording frustrate players.
2. Obtaining games and guides, including release mismatches.
3. Giving Muse a real interpreter and hints a little at a time.
4. Capturing labeled synthetic mistakes and real responses.
5. Testing capture and reviewing the meaning of each label.
6. Comparing retrieval baselines and, if warranted, creating a model.
7. Results, limitations and what we would change.

